#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079278 #edb/ref 1061 1239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a31f8558-9f90-529c-bdfb-02f8ea14cdd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079698 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a differentiable function. The graph of the derivative $y=f'(x)$ is given below. Can we determine the intervals on which $f(x)$ is increasing or decreasing?\n\n![](images/5b/5ba6b1945470b0ad98c1d5b6fe909e09759fb55f5ec5444c06079404c53853ad.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we use the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f(x)$ is increasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is positive for every value in $(a,b).$ *\n\nWe also have the following related theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f(x)$ is decreasing on the interval $(a,b)$ if the derivative $f'(x)$ is negative for every value in $(a,b).$ *\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $f'(x) > 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(0,4), \\quad (7,10), \\quad (10,15).\n$$\n\nAlso from the graph, we see that $f'(x) < 0$ on the intervals\n\n$$\n(4,7), \\quad (15, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \\in (0,4) \\cup (7,10) \\cup (10,15)$ and decreasing for $x \\in (4,7) \\cup (15, \\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079698 #edb/ref 1065 644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079698 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fa993118-a893-58bc-848b-51a9de8b0ba4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079698 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079699 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that we can use the first derivative test to identify the relative extrema of a continuous function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *positive* to *negative* around $x=a.$\n\n![](images/99/9912cc68ed61884957907c360f172fe37582827b67e0b9873795ae475dc1e86c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if $f'(x)$ changes its sign from *negative* to *positive* around $x=a.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8da20322f6797554f2c52642fd313df78ce1a7a67f54e3c524af446398ce515.png)\n\nSo, to determine the relative extrema of a function $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to the locations where $f'(x)$ changes sign." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079699 #edb/ref 1065 647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079699 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2a3ce52-dbfc-5cef-a5bf-db4fed3ca603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079699 #edb/ref 1067 "Relative Extrema" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506686 #edb/ref 1080 "t\\in (7,8)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506686 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506686 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04d9f091-3ca5-5ea0-b11a-71932ccb3dd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506698 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506698 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506698 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bfacb6a-e256-5c8e-934f-448424d2d5e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506702 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506702 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506702 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d63fa6a-dfb8-540a-beb5-b67a05ed94a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506704 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506704 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506704 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0dcba3eb-eed2-565c-868b-f2bc3be37bd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506707 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506707 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506707 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e8198e9-9ec4-53dd-9912-c794bdaa85a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506708 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=0$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506708 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506708 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e8c4cdf-b862-56df-9c65-900b8110f30c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1